WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.773
An engineering workstation contains specialized software used to configure, program, diagnose,

00:00:04.773 --> 00:00:06.473
and maintain control-system components.

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:11.146
It may hold controller projects, HMI applications, device profiles, firmware tools,

00:00:11.146 --> 00:00:13.271
network settings, and vendor utilities.

00:00:13.271 --> 00:00:16.245
Because it can make privileged operational changes,

00:00:16.245 --> 00:00:20.918
it is one of the most security-sensitive assets in an ICS.

00:00:20.918 --> 00:00:24.742
Engineering work should follow approved pathways and change procedures.

00:00:24.742 --> 00:00:27.716
Dedicated accounts, controlled removable media, verified software,

00:00:27.716 --> 00:00:31.540
protected project repositories, and known-good backups help preserve integrity.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:34.514
Remote engineering access should be time-limited, monitored,

00:00:34.514 --> 00:00:37.488
and tied to a specific work authorization.

00:00:37.488 --> 00:00:40.037
A historian performs a different role.

00:00:40.037 --> 00:00:44.710
It collects process values, alarms, events, and production information over time.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:48.959
Operators and engineers use trends for troubleshooting and performance analysis,

00:00:48.959 --> 00:00:54.482
while business systems may consume selected data for reporting, planning, or quality functions.

00:00:54.482 --> 00:00:59.156
Historians are high-value because their data reveals how the process behaves.

00:00:59.156 --> 00:01:02.979
They also often connect operational and enterprise information flows.

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:08.078
Architecture should avoid uncontrolled direct access from business networks into control systems.

00:01:08.078 --> 00:01:09.352
Replication, brokered interfaces,

00:01:09.352 --> 00:01:13.601
or services in an industrial DMZ can reduce unnecessary paths.

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:19.124
Access control should separate users who view data from users who administer collection,

00:01:19.124 --> 00:01:20.399
interfaces, or retention.

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:24.647
Monitor changes to tags, collection sources, replication destinations, service identities,

00:01:24.647 --> 00:01:25.922
and time settings.

00:01:25.922 --> 00:01:31.020
Protect historian availability and integrity so incident responders can trust the timeline.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.694
Engineering workstations can change the process; historians can explain the process.

00:01:35.694 --> 00:01:40.367
Both deserve strong inventory, endpoint management, backup, logging, and recovery planning.

00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:46.740
Security teams should preserve their specialized functions rather than treating them as interchangeable office computers.
